                       THE MAYLAND CONFIG, IN FULL

Nothing reads this file. It is here because `msh` has `cat` and this machine
has no manual pages and no way to reach the internet - and because eighty
lines of prose re-parsed on every boot and every reload is eighty lines that
belong somewhere else.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHERE THE CONFIG LIVES

    /etc/mayland/default.d/*.conf   what the build ships
    /etc/mayland/may.conf           this machine's own file
    /etc/mayland/conf.d/*.conf      yours; nothing is ever installed here

Read in that order, in the order of their names inside a directory, and the
later line wins. Authority rises down the list without exception - which is
the one place this differs from systemd, where the main file is read *first*
and a drop-in shipped by a package can override what you wrote by hand.

Only `*.conf` is read. A file left behind by an editor joins nothing.

A file in conf.d with the same name as one in default.d REPLACES it: the
shipped one is not read at all. An empty file is therefore an off switch.
`systemd` spells the same thing as a symlink to /dev/null.

Without any of these files - a disk that was never mounted - `/bin/maywm`
comes up with the bindings compiled into it, a shorter version of what
default.d ships, so a machine still has a desktop somebody can drive.

At startup and after every reload, the console lists every file that was
read. That is `systemd-analyze cat-config`, except that it is not a second
program and it happens at the moment it matters.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT A LINE MAY SAY

  $name = value      a variable. Collected from EVERY file before any line is
                     dispatched, so it may be defined in a file read after the
                     one that uses it, and the most authoritative layer wins.
                     Its value may not contain another variable - that is what
                     keeps a cycle unwritable rather than detected
  source = <path>    another config, read in place, up to four deep. The path
                     is a path, not a template: `$` in it is not expanded,
                     because the list of files must be complete before any
                     variable is known
  <list> =           a list keyword with nothing after it CLEARS the list.
                     bind, windowrule, animation, bezier, exec-once, and the
                     panel's modules-left / modules-center / modules-right
  exec-once = <cmd>  run once, after the desktop is up. Arguments allowed
  monitor = NAME, MODE, POSITION, SCALE[, vrr, 0|1][, bitdepth, 8|10]
                     Hyprland's line, unchanged. NAME is the connector as
                     Linux names it (`display` lists them: DP-3, HDMI-A-1,
                     Virtual-1), `desc:` and the start of the monitor's
                     description, or nothing for every other output. MODE is
                     preferred, highrr, highres, maxwidth or WxH[@R] - the
                     closest of the monitor's own modes, width first, then
                     height, then refresh; no @ means 60 Hz, as in Hyprland.
                     POSITION is auto or XxY (one output, so it is kept and
                     not used); SCALE is 1 or auto. `monitor = NAME, disable`
                     is read, and said to have no effect yet.
                     Put on when the desktop comes up, on reload and when a
                     monitor is plugged in - not on every change, so a
                     `display mode` typed by hand stays until then.
                     transform, mirror, cm, sdr*, icc and other scales are
                     refused by name. No monitor line: the outputs stay as
                     the boot made them
  misc { vrr = 0|1 } variable refresh for every output whose monitor line does
                     not say. 2 and 3 (fullscreen only) are refused

  theme        page, card, accent, on_accent, text, text_muted, border
               the one block both programs read
    wallpaper = <path>
               the picture behind everything; no line at all means the
               gradient. It must be a QOI - the machine decodes exactly one
               image format, because that decoder is ninety lines and a PNG
               decoder is an inflate implementation with a picture attached.
               The build converts anything in `assets/wallpapers/` into
               /etc/mayland/wallpapers/<name>.qoi, so drop a JPEG in there and
               name the result here. It is scaled to cover the screen and
               cropped evenly, which is what a wallpaper wants.

  animations   enabled
    bezier = <name>, <x1>, <y1>, <x2>, <y2>
    animation = <class>, <on>, <speed>, <curve>[, <style>]
      class    windows windowsIn windowsOut layers layersIn layersOut
               fade workspaces
      speed    hundreds of milliseconds, so 4 is 400 ms
      style    slide | fade | popin <n>%
      fade and popin need the shader pass, so they want decoration.blur on;
      slide works wherever the desktop is composed at all

  panel        height, position = top | bottom, workspaces
               modules-left, modules-center, modules-right
    modules    workspaces window clock mem net uptime

  general      gaps_in, gaps_out, layout = master | dwindle | float
               border_size, col.active_border, col.inactive_border
  decoration   rounding, active_opacity, shadow_strength, shadow_range
  decoration.blur   enabled, size
  dwindle      split_ratio
  master       mfact
  input        kb_layout = us | ru | us,ru (up to four, in switching order)
               kb_options = grp:alt_shift_toggle (or nothing: no switch)
               repeat_rate, repeat_delay, follow_mouse, sensitivity,
               natural_scroll

  submap = <name> ... submap = reset
    the lines between belong to that map, and it is bound instead of the
    default one - so a config may have more bindings than the sixty-four
    the compositor holds at once

  bind[flags] = <mods>, <key>, <dispatcher>[, <argument>]
    flags        e repeat while held   r on release   n do not swallow
                 i ignore other mods   l accepted, there is no lock screen
                 m mouse, which is only the key being a button
    mods         SUPER SHIFT CTRL ALT, spaces or plus signs between them
    key          a character; Return Escape Tab Space Backspace;
                 F1..F12; Up Down Left Right Home End PageUp PageDown
                 Insert Delete; minus equal comma period slash semicolon
                 apostrophe grave bracketleft bracketright backslash;
                 mouse:272 and the rest, or mouse_left mouse_right
    dispatcher   killactive closewindow killwindow
                 workspace movetoworkspace movetoworkspacesilent
                 movefocus cyclenext focuswindow focuswindowbyclass
                 focuscurrentorlast swapwindow swapnext
                 togglefloating setfloating settiled fullscreen pin
                 moveactive resizeactive centerwindow splitratio layout
                 movewindow resizewindow movecursor movecursortocorner
                 submap reload exec execr
    a workspace argument may be a number, +1 / -1, e+1 / e-1 (counting only
    workspaces with something on them), or `previous`

  windowrule = <what>, class:<pattern> | title:<pattern>
    what         float tile fullscreen pin nofocus noblur blur center
                 opacity <n>, rounding <n>, workspace <n>
                 size <w> <h>, move <x> <y>
    the pattern is a substring, so `class:term` catches `xterm`

  A colour is written rgb(), rgba() or 0x - never as #rrggbb, because a hash
  starts a comment here as it does in hyprland.conf, and `page = #0f0d13` is
  the line `page =` with nothing after it.

  rgba(RRGGBBAA) means see-through, not darker. The bar is a surface the
  compositor blends over the wallpaper, so `card = rgba(211f24bf)` is a pane
  with the desktop behind it. A colour written without an alpha is solid, so
  `card = rgb(211f24)` is the bar it always was. Two things follow:

    - the alpha of `card` is the alpha of the whole bar, because the pills on
      it are shades of it and inherit what it says
    - `col.active_border` and `col.inactive_border` accept an alpha and do not
      use it. A window's ring is put on the screen by the copy engine, which
      cannot blend, and a config that quietly meant something else would be
      worse than one that says this out loud

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT IT SAYS BACK

Every complaint names its file and its line:

    maywm: /etc/mayland/conf.d/99-mine.conf:4: this desktop has no such
           setting - gaps_inn

A section this desktop does not have - cursor, debug, group, gestures, and
every key of misc but vrr - says so once rather than once a line, and a
keyword it does not have - env, layerrule, plugin - says so once as well.

A monitor line about an output that is not there, or has nothing plugged
in, says so with the outputs that are:

    maywm: monitor DP-2: nothing is plugged into it - connected: DP-3

and the configuration it sent, or why it was refused:

    maywm: outputs: DP-3 1920x1080@200

Two files of the SAME layer setting one thing is a mistake nobody can see, so
it is said out loud:

    maywm: /etc/mayland/conf.d/99-mine.conf:4: this setting -
           already set in /etc/mayland/conf.d/20-look.conf:7

A higher layer overriding a lower one is silent. That is what layers are for.
